// CARD a1-01 · SEE: a real map of Billund zooming in; on "Billund" a red pin drops on the town; then a circle opens from the pin into the real red-brick house Ole Kirk built on Billund's main street · SEAM in right
# cine act=setup fx="code:@map_dive;map:lego_map_billund;x:.48;y:.69;go:Danish;iris:Billund;photo:lego_ole_kirks_house_01;lab:BILLUND;sub:OLE KIRK'S HOUSE · BILLUND;in:right"
It started with wood, in a carpenter's workshop in the Danish village of **Billund**.

// CARD a1-02 · SEE: a carpenter's hands planing a board, shavings curling; on "Ole" his real 1957 portrait slides in as an old print from the left and settles · SEAM out right
# cine act=setup fx="code:@print_slide;clip:lego_clip_planing_shavings;pic:lego_ole_kirk_portrait;go:Ole;side:left;cap:OLE KIRK CHRISTIANSEN;sub:carpenter · from 1932;out:right"
In nineteen thirty-two, Ole Kirk Christiansen began making wooden **toys**.

// CARD a1-03 · SEE: the real 1936 LEGO logo dim behind; LEG and GODT slide in from both sides; on "well" the D and T drop out of the line and the rest closes up into LEGO, in gold; play well sets under it · SEAM in right
# cine act=setup fx="code:@word_join;bg:lego_logo_1936;a:LEG;b:GODT;keep:0,1,4,5;go:well;gloss:leg godt · play well;in:right"
He took the name from two Danish words, leg godt: play **well**. (J)

// CARD a1-04 · SEE: a chisel and mallet working pale wood in close-up; the motto sets in the world above the hands · SEAM out right
# cine act=setup fx="code:@reel;clip:lego_clip_chisel_closeup;move:in;z:.18;txt:“ONLY THE BEST IS GOOD ENOUGH”;sub:the motto, 1936;tat:motto;ty:.18;out:right"
His motto, from nineteen thirty-six: only the best is good **enough**.

// CARD a1-05 · SEE: a hand plane gliding along a board behind; a split-flap calendar runs from JAN 1932 to FEB 1960 on "first" and locks; 28 YEARS OF WOOD sets under it · SEAM in right
# cine act=setup fx="code:@month_flip;from:1932-01;to:1960-02;go:first;lab:28 YEARS OF WOOD;sub:wooden toys, 1932 → 1960;bg:lego_clip_planer_closeup;in:right"
For its first twenty-eight years, LEGO was a wood **company**.

// CARD a1-06 · SEE: white plastic granules pouring from a sack into a growing heap, the camera pushing in · SEAM out right
# cine act=setup fx="code:@reel;clip:lego_clip_plastic_granules;move:in;z:.2;out:right"
Plastic came after the war, and LEGO's early bricks followed a British design, Kiddicraft's self-locking **brick**.

// CARD a1-07 · SEE: a whole Christmas town built on green baseplates, the camera travelling across it house by house: every set part of one world · SEAM in right
# cine act=setup fx="code:@reel;under:lego_minifig_christmas;ua:0;move:right;z:.4;in:right"
{warm} What LEGO added wasn't the brick. It was a **system**.

// CARD a1-08 · SEE: a miniature village with a model train running through it; on "Godtfred" his real 1950 portrait slides in from the right as a print · SEAM out right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@print_slide;clip:lego_clip_miniature_village_train;pic:lego_godtfred_cropped;go:Godtfred;side:right;cap:GODTFRED KIRK CHRISTIANSEN;sub:the System of Play · 1955;out:right"
In nineteen fifty-five, Ole's son Godtfred launched the System of **Play**.

// CARD a1-09 · SEE: the real 1957 photograph of a boy building with red and white bricks at a table, the camera pushing in to his hands · SEAM in right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@reel;under:lego_brick_1957_early;ua:0;move:in;z:.35;in:right"
So every new set would work with every set a child already **owned**.

// CARD a1-10 · SEE: a hand easing a block out of a tall wobbling tower; it leans and nearly goes · SEAM out right
# cine act=doubt fx="code:@reel;clip:lego_clip_jenga_pull;move:in;z:.15;out:right"
But the bricks didn't grip yet. The fix came in nineteen fifty-**eight**.

// CARD a1-11 · SEE: the real patent pages flick past like a thumbed book and stop on the sheet of brick sections; on "tubes" the camera reads into FIG. 8 and a highlighter covers the round tubes inside the brick · SEAM in right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@page_turn;pages:lego_doc_patent_us3005282_text,lego_doc_patent_us3005282_drawing_sheet1,lego_doc_patent_us3005282_drawing_sheet2;stop:patent;marks:.52,.23,.73,.32@tubes;zoom:tubes;z:1.2;lab:FILED 1958;in:right"
On the twenty-eighth of January, LEGO filed a patent: tubes inside the brick, so it **clutches**.

// CARD a1-12 · SEE: Ole Kirk's portrait framed on a dark wall over his name plate; on "died" the photo drains grey and slides out of the frame; 11 MARCH 1958 sets beside it · SEAM hard cut · HOLD
# cine act=fall fx="code:@photo_drain;pic:lego_ole_kirk_1957;plate:OLE KIRK;go:died;date:11 MARCH 1958"
Ole Kirk died a few weeks **later**.

// CARD a1-13 · SEE: the real early wooden LEGO toys in their case; on "fire" the photo burns away from a point with a glowing edge, revealing an empty workshop · SEAM out right
# cine act=fall fx="code:@burn_away;a:lego_wooden_toys_history;b:lego_art_workshop_dawn;go:fire;out:right"
In February nineteen sixty, a fire hit the wooden-toy **workshop**.

// CARD a1-14 · SEE: hands sweeping wood dust off a bench, the last of the wood · SEAM in right
# cine act=fall fx="code:@reel;clip:lego_clip_sweeping_dust;move:in;z:.18;in:right"
{grave} Godtfred stopped making wooden toys, and his brothers **left**.

// CARD a1-15 · SEE: a steel injection press closing over red mould pieces, again and again · SEAM out right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@reel;clip:lego_clip_injection_machine;move:in;z:.2;out:right"
From then on LEGO made one thing: plastic bricks, in limited shapes and limited **colours**.

// CARD a1-16 · MOTIF · SEE: a grey studded baseplate with a few bricks on it; the brick drops in from above and clicks onto the plate; LIMITED SHAPES · LIMITED COLOURS sets above · SEAM in right
# cine act=lesson fx="code:@brick_drop;bg:lego_duplo_stacked;go:limit;x:.32;y:.62;h:.12;lab:LIMITED SHAPES · LIMITED COLOURS;lx:.5;z:.25;move:right;in:right"
Remember that limit. It's what made the system **work**.
